s/muhammad-ahmad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ahmad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ahmad was a Sudanese Sufi teacher who in 1881 declared himself the Mahdi and led a revolt against Turco-Egyptian rule in the Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-al-amin-bey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Amin Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Amin Bey, known as Lamine Bey, was the last ruler of the Husainid dynasty of Tunisia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-ayyashi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Ayyashi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Ayyashi was a Moroccan Sufi warrior who led holy war against the Spanish coastal garrisons in the early 17th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-al-badr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Badr</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Badr was the last Zaydi imam and king of the Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-fasi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Fasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Fasi was a sharif, said to have come from Morocco, who around 1550 established himself in the Fezzan oases of southern Libya and founded the Awlad Muhammad…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-habib-of-trarza"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Habib of Trarza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Habib was emir of Trarza in south-western Mauritania from 1827 until 1860.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Idrisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Idrisi was an Arab geographer and cartographer, born probably in Ceuta, who worked at the court of the Norman king Roger II of Sicily in Palermo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-khalili"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Khalili</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad bin Abdullah al-Khalili was the Ibadi Imam of Oman from 1920 until his death in 1954, ruling the interior from Nizwa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-muzaffar-of-badajoz"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Muzaffar of Badajoz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Muzaffar of Badajoz was the second Aftasid ruler of the Taifa of Badajoz, reigning from about 1045 to 1068.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-al-nasir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Nasir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Nasir was Almohad caliph from 1199 to 1213, son of Yaqub al-Mansur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-qaim"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Qa'im</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Qa'im bi-amr Allah was the founder of the Saadi dynasty of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-sanusi-of-dar-al-kuti"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Sanusi of Dar al-Kuti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Sanusi of Dar al-Kuti was sultan of Dar al-Kuti, in what is now the north of the Central African Republic, from 1890 to 1911.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-sharif-of-wadai"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Sharif of Wadai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Sharif was sultan of Wadai from 1835 to 1858.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-shaykh-al-wattasi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Shaykh al-Wattasi was the founder of the Wattasid sultanate of Morocco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-tunisi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad al-Tunisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad al-Tunisi was a North African scholar and traveller who lived at the court of the Darfur Sultanate from about 1803 to 1812, under Sultan Mohammed al-Fadl.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-al-abid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ali-al-abid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali al-Abid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali al-Abid was the first president of the Syrian Republic under the French mandate, serving from 1932 to 1936.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ali-jinnah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Ali Jinnah was the leader of the All-India Muslim League and the founder of Pakistan.<