i><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-falihi-kaimuddin"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Falihi Kaimuddin was the last sultan of Buton, an island sultanate off south-eastern Sulawesi in Indonesia, reigning from 1937 until his death in 1960.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haidar-dughlat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-haidar-dughlat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Haidar Dughlat was a Central Asian prince, soldier and historian of the Dughlat family and a cousin of the Mughal emperor Babur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal was a Somali politician who became prime minister of the State of Somaliland at independence in June 1960 and later prime minister of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-husayn-khan-of-derbent"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Husayn Khan of Derbent</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Husayn Khan of Derbent was the first khan of Derbent, the walled Caspian city in Dagestan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-al-mustansir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-i-al-mustansir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I al-Mustansir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I al-Mustansir was the Hafsid ruler of Ifriqiya from 1249 to 1277 and the first of the dynasty to take the title of caliph, in 1253.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-i-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I of Granada, known as Ibn al-Ahmar, was the founder of the Nasrid dynasty and the Emirate of Granada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-tapar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-i-tapar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad I Tapar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad I Tapar was sultan of the Seljuk Empire from 1105 to 1118, son of Malik-Shah I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-aid"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn A'id</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn A'id was the last independent emir of Asir in south-western Arabia, from the Al A'id family that ruled from Abha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab was a religious scholar of Najd whose call for a strict return to the oneness of God gave rise to the movement known as Wahhabism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-allah-al-birzali"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Birzali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Birzali was leader of the Banu Birzal, a Berber clan brought to al-Andalus as soldiers by al-Mansur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abil-saj"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Abi'l-Saj</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Abi'l-Saj, also called Afshin, was the founder of the Sajid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi was the founder of the Idrisid Emirate of Asir on the Red Sea coast of Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-senussi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Senussi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Senussi, known as the Grand Senussi, was an Algerian-born scholar who founded the Senussi Sufi order in Mecca in 1837.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-falah"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Falah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Falah was the founder of the Mushasha dynasty of Khuzestan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ibrahim-al-fazari"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Fazari</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Fazari was an astronomer of the early Abbasid period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ilyas"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Ilyas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Ilyas was a Samanid commander of Sogdian origin who made himself ruler of Kerman in south-eastern Iran around 932, founding the Ilyasid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-musafir"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Musafir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Musafir was lord of Tarom in northern Iran and the founder of the Sallarid (Musafirid) dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-muzayn"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Muzayn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Muzayn was the founder of the Taifa of Silves in the western Algarve.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-nasir"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Nasir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Nasir was a ruler of the Adal Sultanate in the late sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-nasir-bu-shentuf"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Nasir Bu Shentuf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Nasir Bu Shentuf was the last important emir of the Hintata, the Masmuda Berber dynasty that ruled Marrakesh in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ibn-qasim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Qasim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Qasim was an Umayyad general who conquered Sind in the lower Indus valley around 711 to 712.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-rashid"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Rashid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Rashid was emir of Jabal Shammar in northern Arabia from 1869 to 1897 and the greatest ruler of the Rashidi dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-said-ibn-harun"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Said ibn Harun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Said ibn Harun was the second and last ruler of the Taifa of Santa Maria del Algarve, the small Muslim principality centred on the town later called Faro.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Saud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Saud was emir of Diriyah in Najd from 1727 and the founder of the first Saudi state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-shaddad"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Shaddad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Shaddad was the founder of the Shaddadid dynasty, a Kurdish family that rose as the Sallarid emirate declined.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-sulayman-emir-of-tlemcen"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Sulayman (emir of Tlemcen)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Sulayman was the founder of the Sulaymanid dynasty of Tlemcen in western Algeria in the early 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-sulayman-al-katib"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Katib</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Katib was an Abbasid commander who ended the Tulunid dynasty in Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-suri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ibn-suri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Suri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Suri was a chieftain of Ghor in central Afghanistan and an ancestor of the Ghurid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tahir"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Tahir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Tahir was the last Tahirid governor of Khurasan, ruling from Nishapur from 862 to 873 on behalf of the Abbasid caliphs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ibn-tughj-al-ikhshid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Tughj al-Ikhshid was a Turkic commander of Fergana origin who became governor of Egypt in 935 and founded the Ikhshidid dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-uthman-al-mazrui"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Mazrui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Mazrui was the Omani governor of Mombasa who made the city independent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-wasif"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Wasif</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Wasif was a secretary at the court of the Saffarid ruler Ya'qub ibn al-Layth in Sistan in the late 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yahya-al-yahsubi"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Yahsubi was ruler of the Taifa of Niebla, in the far south-west of al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yahya-hamid-al-din"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Yahya Hamid al-Din</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Yahya Hamid al-Din, who ruled as Imam al-Mansur Muhammad, was the Zaydi imam of Yemen from 1890 to 1904 and the founder of the Hamid al-Din dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yusuf-al-ukhaydir"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Ukhaydir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Ukhaydir was the founder of the Ukhaydirid dynasty of al-Yamama in central Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-zayd"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Zayd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Zayd was the second ruler of the Alavid (Zaydi) state in Tabaristan, succeeding his brother Hasan ibn Zayd in 884.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ziyad"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad ibn Ziyad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad ibn Ziyad was the founder of the Ziyadid dynasty of Yemen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-seljuk-sultan-of-iraq"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad II (Seljuk sultan of Iraq)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad II, son of Mahmud II, was Seljuk sultan of Iraq and western Iran from 1153 to 1159.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-cordoba"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad II of Córdoba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad II al-Mahdi was an Umayyad caliph of Córdoba who seized power in a revolt in February 1009, deposing Hisham II and overthrowing the Amirid regime of Sanchuelo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-granada"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad II of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad II was the second Nasrid sultan of Granada, reigning from 1273 to 1302.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-kelantan"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad II of Kelantan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad II of Kelantan was sultan of Kelantan, on the east coast of the Malay Peninsula, from 1837 to 1886.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad III as-Sadiq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad III as-Sadiq was bey of Tunis of the Husainid dynasty from 1859 to 1882.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-of-granada"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad III of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad III was the third Nasrid emir of Granada, reigning from 1302 to 1309.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-iqbal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Iqbal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Muhammad Iqbal was an Islamic philosopher and poet of British India, among the most influential Muslim thinkers of the 20th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iv-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-iv-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad IV of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad IV was Nasrid emir of Granada from 1325 to 1333, coming to the throne as a boy of ten.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ix-of-granada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-ix-of-granada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad IX of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad IX, called al-Aysar (the Left-handed), was Nasrid emir of Granada four times between 1419 and 1453.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-khan-bangash"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-khan-bangash.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Khan Bangash</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Khan Bangash was an Afghan soldier of fortune who founded the Bangash nawabs of Farrukhabad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-naguib"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-naguib.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Naguib</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Naguib was an Egyptian army officer who served as the figurehead of the Free Officers' coup of July 1952 that forced King Farouk to abdicate.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-nazim-khan"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Nazim Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Nazim Khan was mir of Hunza in the Karakoram from 1892 until his death in 1938.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-of-ghor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad of Ghor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad of Ghor was a ruler of the Ghurid dynasty who conquered much of northern India in the late twelfth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-pulalun"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Pulalun</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Pulalun was Sultan of Sulu in the mid-nineteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-quli-qutb-shah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Quli Qutb Shah was the fifth sultan of Golconda, reigning from 1580 to 1612.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rahim-of-bukhara"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Rahim of Bukhara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Rahim of Bukhara was a chief of the Manghit tribe who took effective power in the Khanate of Bukhara in the 1740s under the protection of Nader Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Rumfa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Rumfa was ruler of the Hausa kingdom of Kano from about 1463 to 1499.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-salahuddin-of-bima"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Salahuddin of Bima</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Salahuddin of Bima was the last sultan of Bima, on the island of Sumbawa in Indonesia, reigning from 1915 to 1951.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-mughal-emperor"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Shah (Mughal emperor)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Shah was Mughal emperor from 1719 to 1748.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-iii-bahmani"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Shah III Bahmani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Shah III was sultan of the Bahmani Sultanate from 1463 to 1482, succeeding his brother Nizam Shah as a boy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-of-brunei"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Shah of Brunei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Shah was, by Brunei tradition, the first Muslim sultan of Brunei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shah-of-pahang"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Shah of Pahang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Shah was the first sultan of Pahang on the east coast of the Malay Peninsula.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-shaybani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Muhammad Shaybani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Muhammad Shaybani was the leader of the Uzbeks who conquered Transoxiana and Khorasan in the early sixteenth century.</span></a></li></ul>
